# Cloudflare Workers Static Assets header rules.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ALONGSIDE next.config.ts
# ---------------------------------------------
# `headers()` in next.config.ts is applied by the Next server. On Cloudflare
# the contents of public/ are served by the ASSETS binding at the edge and the
# Worker is never woken (see "What runs where" in CLOUDFLARE.md), so that rule
# cannot reach /sw.js there. The Docker/standalone deployment is the opposite:
# the Next server serves public/, so next.config.ts is what applies and this
# file is inert — and, being an ordinary file in public/, is also readable at
# /_headers there. It carries no secrets, so that costs nothing. Each
# deployment needs its own copy of the same rule, and both have to say the
# same thing.
#
# The rule itself: a service worker must never be served from a cache, or a
# deploy ships a new worker that nobody's browser asks for — the old one keeps
# answering from the old caches, and the VERSION bump inside sw.js means
# nothing. Everything else in public/ keeps Cloudflare's defaults.

/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
  Service-Worker-Allowed: /
